    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, it was the most durable kingdom to emerge from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> and the common ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>’s Catholic baptism allied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> with the church; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> later allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, which crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> emperor in 800.</li><li>Kings divided the realm among their sons, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 split it into West, Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Carolingian reckoning of 12 pennies to the shilling and 240 to the pound survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s pounds, shillings and pence until decimalisation in 1971.</p></aside>
    </section>

