    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/childeric-i.jpg" alt="Childeric I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i">Childeric I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHILDERICI_REGIS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> who appear in Roman sources from the third century on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and by the fifth century groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> had settled in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, often as allies of the empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i">Childeric I</a>, whose richly furnished grave was discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> in 1653, ruled one of these groups and cooperated with Roman commanders. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> succeeded him, traditionally in 481. In 486 Clovis defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a>, the Roman ruler of the Domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, and took over northern Gaul as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. He then defeated the Alemanni and, at a date disputed between about 496 and 508, was baptised a Catholic Christian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-remigius">Remigius</a>, rather than adopting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arian Christianity</a> of the other Germanic kings. This choice won him the support of the Gallo-Roman bishops. In 507 he defeated and killed the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii">Alaric II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a>, adding most of Aquitaine to his realm. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius</a> granted him an honorary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, and in his last years Clovis eliminated the rulers of the other Frankish groups, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigibert-the-lame">Sigibert of Cologne</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> his chief residence and summoned a church council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> in 511, the year he died.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/luxeuil-abbey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxeuil-abbey.jpg" alt="Luxeuil Abbey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/luxeuil-abbey">Luxeuil Abbey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-08_-_Monast%C3%A8re_de_Luxeuil_-_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.BourgeoisP, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> was overwhelmingly rural. Most people worked the land as free peasants, dependent tenants or slaves on estates belonging to the king, the church and a landed aristocracy in which Frankish and Gallo-Roman families gradually merged. Roman cities survived mainly as seats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, evolving towards early Romance, remained the language of most of the population west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, while Frankish speech persisted in the north-east. The church grew immensely rich through royal and aristocratic gifts, and monasteries multiplied, especially after the Irish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxeuil-abbey">Luxeuil</a> around 590; the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a> was buried in 639, became closely tied to the crown. Long-distance trade continued through Marseille in the sixth and seventh centuries, while from the seventh century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> trading places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quentovic">Quentovic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorestad">Dorestad</a> linked the Frankish lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The Merovingian gold coinage gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> penny, or <em>denier</em>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> standardised in a system of 240 pennies to the pound. Carolingian estate surveys, such as the polyptych of the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a> compiled under Abbot Irminon, show large manors on which tenants owed rents and labour services on the lord’s demesne.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clovis-i.jpg" alt="Clovis I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Remy_baptise_Clovis_d%C3%A9tail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pethrus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Frankish armies were raised from the king’s followers and the free landholders, who were obliged to serve at their own expense, together with the armed retinues of counts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots. Early Frankish warriors were known for the throwing axe, the <em>francisca</em>, but by the Carolingian period mounted aristocrats in mail, armed with lance and sword, formed the core of the host, which assembled each spring for the season’s campaign. Conquest drove the kingdom’s growth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>’s sons destroyed the Kingdom of the Thuringians in 531 and the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> in 534 and obtained Provence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in 537, and Frankish armies intervened repeatedly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the sixth century. In the seventh and early eighth centuries the kings lost control of outlying duchies such as Aquitaine, Alamannia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The mayors of the palace restored it by force: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> defeated an Umayyad army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, usually dated 732, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> subdued Aquitaine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard Kingdom</a> in 774, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in campaigns lasting from 772 to 804, broke the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> in the 790s and created the Spanish March, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801, although his rearguard was ambushed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncevaux</a> in 778.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salic-law"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salic-law.jpg" alt="Salic law" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jugement_de_Childebert_III_accordant_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_de_Saint-Denis_la_terre_de_Hodenc-l%27Ev%C3%AAque_dans_l%27Oise.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Merovingian kings treated the realm as a family inheritance, dividing it among their sons, so that it was repeatedly split into kingdoms that came to be known as Austrasia in the east, Neustria in the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and reunited when lines died out, as under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> in 558 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a> in 613. Kings governed through counts, <em>comites</em>, appointed over the old Roman city territories, and through dukes over larger regions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> as powerful local partners. Written law codes such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Pactus Legis Salicae</a></em>, probably first issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>, applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-romans">Gallo-Romans</a> continued to live under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. Chlothar II’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-paris">Edict of Paris</a> of 614 acknowledged the interests of the aristocracy, and from the later seventh century real power passed to the mayors of the palace, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippinids">Pippinid</a> family after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tertry">Battle of Tertry</a> in 687. In 751 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> deposed Childeric III with papal approval and was anointed king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> ruled through assemblies, written ordinances called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a>, travelling royal agents called <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a></em> and the network of counts, and tried to bind the realm together through a common Christian discipline, set out in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admonitio-generalis">Admonitio generalis</a></em> of 789.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral.jpg" alt="Aachen Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen_Germany_Imperial-Cathedral-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CEphoto, Uwe Aranas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> was the most durable of the successor states to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, and it laid the foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>’s Catholic baptism tied Frankish kingship to the Roman church, a link that culminated in the alliance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> anointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> in 754, Pippin’s grant of conquered Italian lands to the pope in 756 laid the basis of the Papal States, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 25 December 800. The Carolingian court sponsored a revival of learning in which scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> corrected texts of the Bible and the classics, schools were attached to cathedrals and monasteries, and the clear script known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a> spread, the ancestor of modern lower-case letters. Many works of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature survive only because they were copied in Carolingian monasteries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> wrote his <em>Histories</em>, the main narrative of the early Merovingian kingdom, in the late sixth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vita-karoli-magni">Life of Charlemagne</a></em> became a model for royal biography. The palace chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> survives as the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a>. The name of the Franks lives on in France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, and later rulers of both France and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> claimed Charlemagne’s inheritance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy.jpg" alt="Battle of Fontenoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Lenfant_-_La_Bataille_de_Fontenoy,_le_11_mai_1745_(Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Versailles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre L'Enfant, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom ended not through conquest but through division. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, sole ruler from 814, issued the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinatio-imperii">Ordinatio imperii</a></em> of 817 to keep the empire united under his eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothar I</a>, but the birth of another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, in 823 and attempts to provide for him provoked rebellions by his elder sons in 830 and 833, when Louis was briefly deposed. After Louis died in 840, his surviving sons Lothar I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and Charles the Bald fought a civil war. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a> in 841 Louis and Charles defeated Lothar, and in 842 they confirmed their alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg Oaths</a>, sworn in early forms of Romance and German. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in August 843 divided the realm into three kingdoms: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> under Charles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> under Louis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, under Lothar, who kept the imperial title. The brothers still saw themselves as rulers of a single Frankish people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> briefly reunited the kingdoms in the 880s, but the division of 843 proved lasting. West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> developed into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and East Francia into the German kingdom, while the middle lands of Lotharingia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Italy remained contested for centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> was the most lasting of the kingdoms built on the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>’s alliance with the Catholic church, the Carolingian partnership with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s empire and revival of learning shaped medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its division in 843 created the frames of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</em></p>

